265
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:26,780
colour, the beauty of the setting,

266
00:20:26,780 --> 00:20:30,280
the whole structure, it's marvellously theatrical and

267
00:20:30,280 --> 00:20:31,280
beautiful.

268
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:32,780
A. And for the moment it

269
00:20:32,780 --> 00:20:34,280
appears that we have heaven on earth.

270
00:20:34,280 --> 00:20:36,280
K. Yes, that's tremendously stimulating.

271
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:38,280
A. But then we have to go out again.

272
00:20:38,280 --> 00:20:41,780
And it's all stimulated through tradition,

273
00:20:41,780 --> 00:20:45,280
through usage of words, chants and certain

274
00:20:45,280 --> 00:20:49,780
association of words, symbols, images, flowers,

275
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:54,280
incense, all that is very, very stimulating.

276
00:20:54,280 --> 00:21:00,280
And if one is used to that, one misses it.

277
00:21:00,280 --> 00:21:04,780
Oh, yes, yes. I was thinking as you

278
00:21:04,780 --> 00:21:09,280
were saying about, at least to my ear, extraordinarily

279
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:11,780
beautiful a language of Sanskrit, and the

280
00:21:11,780 --> 00:21:14,280
chanting of the Gita and the swaying back

281
00:21:14,280 --> 00:21:17,280
and forth, and then one sits down to study

282
00:21:17,280 --> 00:21:20,280
what the words say and one says to himself,

283
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:24,280
now look, what on earth is going on when

284
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:28,280
we're doing this as over against what the word itself

285
00:21:28,280 --> 00:21:30,780
could disclose. But the seduction that

286
00:21:30,780 --> 00:21:33,280
is available, of course it's self-seduction,

287
00:21:33,280 --> 00:21:35,280
one can't blame the language for being beautiful.

288
00:21:35,280 --> 00:21:36,280
K. No, it's self-diss...

289
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:39,780
A. It's a self-diss... yes. And all this

290
00:21:39,780 --> 00:21:43,280
is encouraged. And the notion I take that you're

291
00:21:43,280 --> 00:21:47,280
suggesting that we look at here is

292
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:51,280
that there's a tremendously invested interest in keeping

293
00:21:51,280 --> 00:21:52,280
this up.

294
00:21:52,280 --> 00:21:53,280
K. Of course.

295
00:21:53,280 --> 00:21:54,280
A. Yes.

296
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:57,280
K. And commercial it is. And if it

297
00:21:57,280 --> 00:22:00,280
is not sustained by the priest, the whole thing

298
00:22:00,280 --> 00:22:06,280
would collapse. So is this a battle to

299
00:22:06,280 --> 00:22:12,280
hold the human being in his appetites, which is

300
00:22:12,280 --> 00:22:16,780
really very frightening when you look at

301
00:22:16,780 --> 00:22:21,280
it. Frightening in the sense, rather disgusting

302
00:22:21,280 --> 00:22:27,280
in one way, exploiting people, and

303
00:22:27,280 --> 00:22:33,280
intrinsically destructive to the human mind.

304
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:39,280
A. Yes, yes. I've had this problem in teaching,

305
00:22:39,280 --> 00:22:45,280
in my classes, in terms of my own discussion

306
00:22:46,280 --> 00:22:50,280
Sometimes it has seemed that maybe the first stanza

307
00:22:50,280 --> 00:22:54,280
of a poem that I will have known by

308
00:22:54,280 --> 00:22:58,780
heart would be appropriate. And so I'll begin to

309
00:22:58,780 --> 00:23:03,280
recite it. And when I get to the end of

310
00:23:03,280 --> 00:23:07,280
it, the expectation has arisen, the ears

311
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:11,280
are there, the bodies are leaning forward, and

312
00:23:11,280 --> 00:23:13,280
I have to stop, you see, and I have to

313
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:15,280
say, well now, you see, we can't go on because

314
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:18,780
you're not listening to what I'm saying,

315
00:23:18,780 --> 00:23:22,280
you're listening to how it's being said. And

316
00:23:22,280 --> 00:23:26,280
if I read it terribly, you would no more

317
00:23:26,280 --> 00:23:30,280
listen to what it is, your disgust would dominate,

318
00:23:30,280 --> 00:23:33,280
just as the pleasure is dominating now. And

319
00:23:33,280 --> 00:23:36,280
students have got after me for not reciting

320
00:23:36,280 --> 00:23:39,780
more poetry. You see, that you would be upset

321
00:23:39,780 --> 00:23:43,280
with that is a perfect sign that you haven't

322
00:23:43,280 --> 00:23:45,780
started to do your work in class yet.

323
00:23:45,780 --> 00:23:48,280
And then we're up against the problem that they

324
00:23:48,280 --> 00:23:53,280
think I'm being ascetical and denying the good.

325
00:23:53,280 --> 00:23:56,280
K. Denying the good, yes. Good, quite, quite.

326
00:23:56,280 --> 00:23:57,280
A. That's part of what you...

327
00:23:57,280 --> 00:23:58,280
K. Yes, of course.

328
00:23:58,280 --> 00:23:59,280
A. Good, good. I'm glad

329
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:00,280
you cleared that for me. Yes.

330
00:24:00,280 --> 00:24:03,780
K. And there is this desire, appetite, we know

331
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:07,280
more or less, we have a little bit gone

332
00:24:07,280 --> 00:24:13,780
into it. What is desire? Because I see something,

333
00:24:13,780 --> 00:24:20,280
immediately I must have it. A gown, a coat,

334
00:24:21,280 --> 00:24:29,780
a tie, the feeling of possession, the urge

335
00:24:29,780 --> 00:24:38,280
to acquire, the urge to experience, the urge

336
00:24:38,280 --> 00:24:43,280
to offer an act that will give

337
00:24:43,280 --> 00:24:48,280
me tremendous satisfaction. The satisfaction might be the

338
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:52,780
acquisition, acquiring a tie or a coat or

339
00:24:52,780 --> 00:24:57,280
sleeping with a woman or having a quarry.

340
00:24:57,280 --> 00:25:02,280
Now, behind that isn't there, sir, this desire?

341
00:25:02,280 --> 00:25:07,280
I might desire a house and another might

342
00:25:07,280 --> 00:25:10,780
desire a car, another might desire

343
00:25:10,780 --> 00:25:14,280
to have intellectual knowledge, another might desire

344
00:25:14,280 --> 00:25:20,280
a god or enlightenment. They are all the same.

345
00:25:20,280 --> 00:25:26,280
The objects vary, but the desire is the same.

346
00:25:26,280 --> 00:25:31,280
One I call the noble, the other I

347
00:25:31,280 --> 00:25:36,280
call the ignoble, worldly, stupid. But the desire

348
00:25:36,280 --> 00:25:42,280
is the desire behind it. So what is desire?

349
00:25:42,280 --> 00:25:48,280
How does it come about that this very strong

350
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:55,280
desire is born, is cultured? You follow? What

351
00:25:55,280 --> 00:26:02,280
is desire? How does it take place in each

352
00:26:02,280 --> 00:26:03,280
one of us?

353
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:07,780
A. If I have understood you, you have

354
00:26:07,780 --> 00:26:12,280
made a distinction between on the one hand appetite

355
00:26:12,280 --> 00:26:16,280
associated with natural hunger, that sort of

356
00:26:16,280 --> 00:26:20,280
desire, and now we are talking about desire

357
00:26:20,280 --> 00:26:22,280
which sometimes gets the name artificial. I

358
00:26:22,280 --> 00:26:24,280
don't know whether you would want to call

359
00:26:24,280 --> 00:26:26,280
it that, but sometimes...

360
00:26:26,280 --> 00:26:29,280
Desire. I might desire, but

361
00:26:29,280 --> 00:26:32,280
the objects vary, sir, don't they?

362
00:26:32,280 --> 00:26:33,280
Yes, the objects vary.

363
00:26:33,280 --> 00:26:37,280
Objects of desire vary according to each

364
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:41,280
individual, each tendency and idiosyncrasy or conditioning

365
00:26:41,280 --> 00:26:46,280
and so on. Desire for that and that and that.

366
00:26:46,280 --> 00:26:51,280
But I want to find out what is desire? How

367
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:54,780
does it come about? I

368
00:26:54,780 --> 00:26:58,280
think it is fairly clear that.

369
00:26:58,280 --> 00:27:00,280
You see, sir...

370
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:02,280
A. Do you mean a sense of absence?

371
00:27:02,280 --> 00:27:05,780
K. No, no. I am asking

372
00:27:05,780 --> 00:27:09,280
what is desire? How does it come?

373
00:27:09,280 --> 00:27:12,280
A. One would have to ask himself.

374
00:27:12,280 --> 00:27:18,780
K. Yes, I am asking you how does it come

375
00:27:18,780 --> 00:27:25,280
about that there is this strong desire for or against

376
00:27:25,280 --> 00:27:32,780
desire itself. I think it is clear.

377
00:27:32,780 --> 00:27:40,280
Perception, visual perception, then there is sensation,

378
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:47,280
then there is contact, and desire comes out

379
00:27:47,280 --> 00:27:54,280
of it. That is the process, isn't it?

380
00:27:54,280 --> 00:27:56,780
A. Oh yes, yes. I am quite clear now

381
00:27:56,780 --> 00:27:59,280
what you are saying. I have been listening very hard.

382
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:05,280
K. Perceptions, contact, sensation, desire.

383
00:28:05,280 --> 00:28:08,280
A. And then if the desire is frustrated, anger.

384
00:28:08,280 --> 00:28:13,780
K. All the rest of it follows. So,

385
00:28:13,780 --> 00:28:19,280
desire. So the religious people, monks, throughout the world

386
00:28:19,280 --> 00:28:26,280
said, be without desire, control desire, suppress desire,

387
00:28:26,280 --> 00:28:33,280
or if you cannot, transfer it to something

388
00:28:33,280 --> 00:28:37,780
that is worthwhile, God, or enlightenment,

389
00:28:37,780 --> 00:28:42,280
or truth, or this or that.

390
00:28:42,280 --> 00:28:44,280
A. But then that is just

391
00:28:44,280 --> 00:28:46,280
another form of desire, not to desire.

392
00:28:46,280 --> 00:28:47,280
K. Not to desire, of course.

393
00:28:47,280 --> 00:28:49,280
A. So we never get out of that.

394
00:28:49,280 --> 00:28:53,280
K. But you see, they say control.

395
00:28:53,280 --> 00:28:56,280
A. Yes. Power is brought into play now.

396
00:28:56,280 --> 00:29:06,280
K. Control, desire. Because you need energy to serve God.

397
00:29:06,280 --> 00:29:10,280
And if you are caught in desire,

398
00:29:10,280 --> 00:29:14,280
you are caught in a tribulation, in trouble,

399
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:19,280
which will dissipate your energy, therefore

400
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:24,280
hold it, control it, suppress it.

401
00:29:24,280 --> 00:29:27,280
You have seen this, sir. I

402
00:29:27,280 --> 00:29:30,280
have seen it so often in Rome.

403
00:29:30,280 --> 00:29:34,280
The priests are walking along with the Bible.

404
00:29:34,280 --> 00:29:37,780
And they don't look at anything else, they

405
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:41,280
keep on reading it, because they are attracted.

406
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:43,780
It doesn't matter if it's a woman or a

407
00:29:43,780 --> 00:29:46,280
nice house, or a nice cloth, or so on.

408
00:29:46,280 --> 00:29:52,280
So keep looking at it. Never

409
00:29:52,280 --> 00:29:58,280
expose yourself to tribulation, to temptation.

410
00:29:58,280 --> 00:30:03,780
So hold it, because you

411
00:30:03,780 --> 00:30:09,280
need your energy to serve God.

412
00:30:09,280 --> 00:30:19,280
A. So desire comes about

413
00:30:19,280 --> 00:30:29,280
through visual perception, contact, sensation, desire.

414
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:33,280
That's the process of it.

415
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:37,780
And then there's the whole backlog of memory

416
00:30:37,780 --> 00:30:41,280
of that in the past to reinforce it.

417
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:45,280
I was taken with what you just said.

418
00:30:45,280 --> 00:30:48,280
Here's this book that's already outside me.

419
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:50,280
It's really no more than what they

420
00:30:50,280 --> 00:30:52,280
put on horses when they're on a race.

421
00:30:52,280 --> 00:30:57,280
K. Blinkers. The Bible becomes a blinker.

422
00:30:57,280 --> 00:31:05,280
A. The blinking Bible. Yes, I follow that.

423
00:31:05,280 --> 00:31:09,780
But the thing that caught me

424
00:31:09,780 --> 00:31:14,280
was never quietly looking at it.

425
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:18,280
K. That's it, sir. A. The desire itself. Yes.

426
00:31:18,280 --> 00:31:24,280
I walked once behind a group of monks

427
00:31:24,280 --> 00:31:30,280
in India, and they were very serious monks.

428
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:35,280
The elderly monk with his disciples around him, they

429
00:31:35,280 --> 00:31:40,280
were walking up a hill, and I followed them.

430
00:31:40,280 --> 00:31:43,780
They never once looked at the

431
00:31:43,780 --> 00:31:47,280
beauty of the sky, the blue,

432
00:31:47,280 --> 00:31:53,280
the extraordinary blue of the sky in the mountains,

433
00:31:53,280 --> 00:31:56,280
and the blue light of grass and

434
00:31:56,280 --> 00:31:59,280
the trees and the birds and the water.

435
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:03,280
Never once looked around.

436
00:32:03,280 --> 00:32:07,280
They were concerned, and they had bent their head down,

437
00:32:07,280 --> 00:32:10,780
and they were repeating something, which

438
00:32:10,780 --> 00:32:14,280
I happen to know in Sanskrit,

439
00:32:14,280 --> 00:32:23,280
and going along, totally unaware of nature,

440
00:32:23,280 --> 00:32:27,280
totally unaware of the passers-by,

441
00:32:27,280 --> 00:32:30,280
because their whole life has

442
00:32:30,280 --> 00:32:33,280
been spent in controlling desire

443
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:37,780
and concentrating on what they

444
00:32:37,780 --> 00:32:42,280
thought was the way to reality.

445
00:32:42,280 --> 00:32:52,280
So desire there acted as a repressive, limiting process.

446
00:32:52,280 --> 00:32:54,280
A. Of course.

447
00:32:54,280 --> 00:32:56,780
K. Because they were frightened. If

448
00:32:56,780 --> 00:32:59,280
I look, there might be a woman.

449
00:32:59,280 --> 00:33:03,280
I might be tempted.

450
00:33:04,280 --> 00:33:06,280
Cut it.

451
00:33:06,280 --> 00:33:11,280
So, we see what desire is,

452
00:33:11,280 --> 00:33:16,280
and we see what appetite is.

453
00:33:16,280 --> 00:33:19,280
They are similar.

454
00:33:19,280 --> 00:33:23,280
A. Yes. Would you say appetite

455
00:33:23,280 --> 00:33:27,280
was a specific focus of desire?

456
00:33:27,280 --> 00:33:30,280
K. Yes, put it that way.

457
00:33:30,280 --> 00:33:33,280
Yes. But they both go together.

458
00:33:33,280 --> 00:33:34,280
A. Oh, yes.

459
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:40,280
K. They are two different words for the same thing.

460
00:33:41,280 --> 00:33:46,280
Now, a problem arises.

461
00:33:47,280 --> 00:33:54,280
Need there be a control of desire at all?

462
00:33:55,280 --> 00:33:57,280
You follow, sir?

463
00:33:57,280 --> 00:34:01,280
A. Yes, I am asking myself, because in our conversations

464
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:05,280
I have learned that every time you ask a question,

465
00:34:05,280 --> 00:34:11,279
if I take that question and construe

466
00:34:11,279 --> 00:34:17,279
it in terms of a syllogistical relation

467
00:34:17,279 --> 00:34:21,279
to things that have been stated as premises before,

468
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:25,280
I am certainly not going to come to the answer

469
00:34:25,280 --> 00:34:27,780
that is not the right answer,

470
00:34:27,780 --> 00:34:30,280
is over against the wrong answer.

471
00:34:30,280 --> 00:34:32,280
I am not going to come

472
00:34:32,280 --> 00:34:34,280
to the one answer that is needful.

473
00:34:34,280 --> 00:34:36,280
So every time you have asked me this morning,

474
00:34:36,280 --> 00:34:41,280
I have asked myself inside. Yes, please go ahead.

475
00:34:42,280 --> 00:34:47,780
The discipline is a form

476
00:34:47,780 --> 00:34:53,280
of suppression and control of desire.

477
00:34:54,280 --> 00:34:59,280
Religious, sectarian, non-sectarian,

478
00:34:59,280 --> 00:35:04,280
it is all based on that – control.

479
00:35:04,280 --> 00:35:10,280
Control your appetite, control your desires,

480
00:35:10,280 --> 00:35:14,280
control your thought,

481
00:35:14,280 --> 00:35:20,780
and this control gradually squeezes

482
00:35:20,780 --> 00:35:27,280
out the flow of free energy.

483
00:35:27,280 --> 00:35:29,280
I don't know if I can...

484
00:35:29,280 --> 00:35:31,280
A. Oh, yes, yes, yes.

485
00:35:31,280 --> 00:35:38,280
And yet, amazingly, the Upanishads in particular

486
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:41,280
have been interpreted in terms

487
00:35:41,280 --> 00:35:44,280
of tapas as encouraging this control.

488
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:46,280
K. I know, I know.

489
00:35:46,280 --> 00:35:52,280
In India it is something fantastic.

490
00:35:52,280 --> 00:35:54,780
The monks who have come to

491
00:35:54,780 --> 00:35:57,280
see me, they are called sannyasis,

492
00:35:57,280 --> 00:36:02,280
they have come to see me, they are incredible.

493
00:36:02,280 --> 00:36:05,780
If I can tell you, a monk

494
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:09,280
who came to see me some years ago,

495
00:36:09,280 --> 00:36:11,280
quite a young man,

496
00:36:11,280 --> 00:36:15,780
he left his house and home at

497
00:36:15,780 --> 00:36:20,280
the age of 15 to find God,

498
00:36:20,280 --> 00:36:25,280
and he had renounced everything, put on the robe,

499
00:36:25,280 --> 00:36:27,280
and as he began to

500
00:36:27,280 --> 00:36:29,280
grow older, at 18, 19, 20,

501
00:36:29,280 --> 00:36:34,280
his sexual appetite was something burning.

502
00:36:34,280 --> 00:36:37,280
He explained to me how it became intense,

503
00:36:37,280 --> 00:36:41,280
he had taken a vow of

504
00:36:41,280 --> 00:36:45,280
celibacy, as sannyasis do, monks do.

505
00:36:45,280 --> 00:36:47,780
And he said, day after day,

506
00:36:47,780 --> 00:36:50,280
in my dreams, in my walk,

507
00:36:50,280 --> 00:36:53,280
in my going to house, begging,

508
00:36:53,280 --> 00:36:59,280
this thing was becoming so like a fire.

509
00:36:59,280 --> 00:37:02,280
Do you know what he did to control it?

510
00:37:02,280 --> 00:37:03,780
A. No, no. What did he

511
00:37:03,780 --> 00:37:05,280
do? K. He had it operated.

512
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:13,280
A. Oh, for heaven's sake. Is that a fact?

513
00:37:13,280 --> 00:37:15,280
K. That...

514
00:37:15,280 --> 00:37:17,780
K. Sir, no, his urge for

515
00:37:17,780 --> 00:37:20,280
God was so... you follow, sirs?

516
00:37:20,280 --> 00:37:21,280
A. Yes.

517
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:25,280
K. The idea, the idea, not the reality.

518
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:27,280
A. Not the reality, no.

519
00:37:27,280 --> 00:37:30,280
K. Sir, he came to see me.

520
00:37:30,280 --> 00:37:34,280
He had heard several talks which had given that place.

521
00:37:34,280 --> 00:37:37,280
He came to see me in tears.

522
00:37:37,280 --> 00:37:42,280
He said, now what have I done? You follow, sir?

523
00:37:42,280 --> 00:37:44,280
A. Oh, I'm sure. Yes.

524
00:37:44,280 --> 00:37:48,280
K. What have I done to myself?

525
00:37:48,280 --> 00:37:51,280
I cannot repair it.

526
00:37:51,280 --> 00:37:55,280
I cannot grow a new organ.

527
00:37:55,280 --> 00:37:58,280
It's finished.

